#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193526} :activity/id #uuid "7794adca-38bc-547f-8ff4-f5c01e74cd30" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049860 :topic/id #uuid "1941f432-c1c0-5aaa-bdb7-e1fa57c348df" :topic/math-academy-id 3943} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193825 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll use our knowledge of solving radical equations to compute inverses of radical functions. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following radical function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\nTo compute the inverse of $f(x),$ we follow the usual four steps:\n\n**Step 1:** First, we replace $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we swap $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = 3\\sqrt{y} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Next, we solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{y}+2 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{y} & =x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{3}(x-2))}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Finally, we replace $y$ with $f^{-1}(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x - 2)^2\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f(x) \\geq 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "24753060-4992-520a-b948-f4e049858666" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6511 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f7338843-a969-5972-9416-842835e163e7" :step/math-academy-id 39425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193527}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192402 :question/id #uuid "7c99605a-2d55-5d37-b363-2e33fa02575c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10456"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bd2d24-7148-5ea0-938f-3c79b0f3f1dd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/